def sar_convert(comparator, bits):
    """Return the code found by successive approximation.
    comparator(code) is True when the input is at or above the DAC voltage for code."""
    code = 0
    for bit in reversed(range(bits)):
        trial = code | (1 << bit)
        if comparator(trial):
            code = trial
    return code

exercises.solution:
```python
def sar_convert(comparator, bits):
    code = 0
    for bit in reversed(range(bits)):
        trial = code | (1 << bit)
        if comparator(trial):
            code = trial
    return code
```

This is binary search on the codes. The invariant is that the input lies at or above the DAC voltage for `code` (the lower end of the remaining interval), and each comparison halves the interval, so after `bits` comparisons it is a single code. An input below 0 V gives 0 and one above the reference gives all ones, as a real converter's output saturates. The hardware version does the same with a capacitor-array DAC and one comparator, one bit per clock.
